  "question": {
    "html": "<p>A research satellite with a mass of <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"850 \\text{ kg}\"></span> is located at a distance of <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"1.5 \\times 10^7 \\text{ m}\"></span> from the center of a newly discovered spherical planet. At this location, the satellite experiences a gravitational force of <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"2.5 \\times 10^3 \\text{ N}\"></span> from the planet. The radius of the planet is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"7.2 \\times 10^6 \\text{ m}\"></span>.</p><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a\"><p><strong>a</strong> </p>Define gravitational field strength at a point.<p>[1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b\"><p><strong>b</strong> </p>Using the data provided, calculate the mass of the planet.<p>[2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c\"><p><strong>c</strong> </p>State the value of the gravitational potential at an infinite distance from the planet.<p>[1]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"d\"><p><strong>d</strong> </p>Calculate the minimum speed required for a probe to escape the gravitational pull of the planet from its surface.<p>[2]</p></section>"
